Title:
KslDump – BMVD (Bring the Microsoft Vulnerable Driver)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- KslDump is a proof-of-concept/tool that dumps credentials from PPL-protected LSASS by abusing a Microsoft Defender–shipped, Microsoft-signed kernel driver (KslD.sys).  
- It solves the “LSASS is PPL-protected” barrier by obtaining arbitrary kernel/physical memory read primitives via a vulnerable IOCTL interface, avoiding third-party BYOVD drivers and avoiding loading any new driver.  
- The technique hinges on switching the KslD service ImagePath back to an older vulnerable on-disk KslD.sys and bypassing a weak “AllowedProcessName” registry string check.  
- It uses IOCTL subcommands to defeat KASLR (leaking CR3/IDTR) and to call MmCopyMemory with attacker-controlled parameters for kernel/physical reads, then walks kernel structures to locate lsass.exe and extract/decrypt credential material.  
- Useful for red teams and security researchers evaluating post-admin credential theft, PPL bypasses, and “living-off-the-land” kernel abuse; also relevant to defenders for understanding gaps in driver blocklists and tamper controls.  
- Interesting because it demonstrates a BYOVD-like outcome using only Microsoft components, and highlights that Microsoft’s vulnerable driver blocklist excludes Microsoft-developed drivers by design.

Technical Focus:
- Microsoft Defender KslD.sys IOCTL abuse (0x222044) and subcommand primitives
- Physical memory read via MmCopyMemory as a PPL bypass
- KASLR defeat via CR3/IDTR leakage and IDT-based ntoskrnl base discovery
- Kernel structure walking (EPROCESS, ActiveProcessLinks) to find LSASS and DTB
- Page table walking using LSASS DTB to read LSASS memory
- LSA secrets/credential extraction and decryption (AES/3DES, BCRYPT chain)

Use Cases:
- Dumping LSASS credentials/hashes on systems where LSASS runs as PPL (post-local-admin)
- Researching/validating “Microsoft-signed BYOVD” style attack paths and driver hygiene issues
- Purple-team testing of detections around Defender driver service tampering (ImagePath/registry edits, service restarts)
- Assessing exposure from leftover vulnerable binaries after platform updates and servicing behavior

Keywords:
KslDump, KslD.sys, Microsoft Defender, BYOVD, BMVD, LSASS, PPL, Protected Process Light, MmCopyMemory, IOCTL 0x222044, physical memory read, kernel memory read, KASLR bypass, CR3, IDTR, IDT, ntoskrnl, EPROCESS, ActiveProcessLinks, DriverSiPolicy.p7b, vulnerable driver blocklist, registry tampering, ImagePath, AllowedProcessName, credential dumping, BCRYPT, AES, 3DES